WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.357
Welcome to Module 5 of the
Attribute-Driven Migration learning path:

2
00:00:04.357 --> 00:00:06.850
build machine configuration packages.

3
00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.532
This module serves Gate 5 of
the implementation guide,

4
00:00:10.532 --> 00:00:14.950
and takes Chapter 07 of Building
the Attribute-Driven Migration.

5
00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.437
For the server settings
machine configuration owns,

6
00:00:18.437 --> 00:00:22.400
the code is a DSC configuration,
and Azure Policy delivers it.

7
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.045
You'll follow Learn's steps, see
where their outputs fall short,

8
00:00:26.045 --> 00:00:28.689
and learn the build that
checks between them:

9
00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:32.548
it cleans the MOF so it's the same
every time, checks the package and

10
00:00:32.548 --> 00:00:36.550
the definition, and refuses what
the release registry doesn't allow.

11
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.075
The lab runs offline on Windows,
with nothing signed in.

12
00:00:40.075 --> 00:00:43.246
The code is illustrative:
tested against fixtures,

13
00:00:43.246 --> 00:00:46.090
not against a production subscription.

14
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.828
Why does a package need a checked build?

15
00:00:51.828 --> 00:00:55.203
Gate 5 sets the conventions
the server track builds on.

16
00:00:55.203 --> 00:00:58.688
Once connected, an Arc server
is an Azure resource whose

17
00:00:58.688 --> 00:01:02.477
tags are its attributes, and each
setting has one owning tool.

18
00:01:02.477 --> 00:01:05.506
For the settings machine
configuration owns,

19
00:01:05.506 --> 00:01:09.491
the code is a DSC configuration,
and Azure Policy delivers it.

20
00:01:09.491 --> 00:01:12.440
Learn's cmdlets build a
package and a definition.

21
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.485
But they don't make the same bytes twice,

22
00:01:14.485 --> 00:01:18.703
and they write your user name and computer
name into what the machines download.

23
00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:21.378
A package is the server
baseline in a file.

24
00:01:21.378 --> 00:01:25.290
So what was built, from what,
and what shipped is the evidence.

25
00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.490
In this module you follow Learn's steps,

26
00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.310
then run the build that
checks between them.

27
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.875
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:36.875 --> 00:01:39.498
which takes about forty-five
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:39.498 --> 00:01:43.541
You'll describe a package, a policy
definition and a guest assignment,

30
00:01:43.541 --> 00:01:45.385
and the three assignment types.

31
00:01:45.385 --> 00:01:48.133
You'll explain what Learn's
cmdlets leave to you,

32
00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:50.810
and why each gap matters
in a federal pipeline.

33
00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:56.297
You'll explain the release registry, and
what MC-POL oh six and oh eight refuse.

34
00:01:56.297 --> 00:01:59.748
You'll run the checked build,
and compare two builds' hashes.

35
00:01:59.748 --> 00:02:02.523
And you'll explain why
the include Arc machines

36
00:02:02.523 --> 00:02:05.298
parameter has to be set
for Arc-enabled servers.

37
00:02:05.298 --> 00:02:09.448
Before you start, do Module 4,
for one owning tool per setting.

38
00:02:09.448 --> 00:02:14.223
You need Windows, with PowerShell
7.1 or later and Pester 5.

39
00:02:14.223 --> 00:02:17.437
The lab's first step saves
three pinned modules from the

40
00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:21.420
PowerShell Gallery into a folder,
rather than installing them.

41
00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:29.090
Microsoft Learn's overview, What
is Azure Machine Configuration,

42
00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.195
describes a model with three parts.

43
00:02:31.195 --> 00:02:36.932
A package is a zip file with the compiled
MOF and the DSC resource modules it uses.

44
00:02:36.932 --> 00:02:38.970
It says what the machine should look like.

45
00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:43.087
A policy definition, in the
Guest Configuration category,

46
00:02:43.087 --> 00:02:45.832
points at the package by URI and by hash.

47
00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:47.771
When the definition is assigned,

48
00:02:47.771 --> 00:02:52.367
the service creates a guest assignment
on each machine in scope, and the agent

49
00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:56.820
on the machine downloads the package,
evaluates it and reports compliance.

50
00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.795
A machine can hold up to
fifty guest assignments.

51
00:02:59.795 --> 00:03:04.207
Learn's page on remediation options
gives each assignment one of three types.

52
00:03:04.207 --> 00:03:06.395
Audit reports and changes nothing.

53
00:03:06.395 --> 00:03:11.033
Apply and monitor applies once, then
reports drift without correcting it.

54
00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:15.746
Apply and auto correct applies, and
corrects drift at the next evaluation.

55
00:03:15.746 --> 00:03:17.720
And the spelling is case sensitive.

56
00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.360
Remember that for step six of the lab.

57
00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:30.004
Microsoft Learn's guide, How to develop
a custom machine configuration package,

58
00:03:30.004 --> 00:03:34.380
documents the whole procedure,
one page a step: set up, author,

59
00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.935
package, test, publish, sign,
define, and publish the definition.

60
00:03:38.935 --> 00:03:42.018
Running those cmdlets offline
shows what they don't do;

61
00:03:42.018 --> 00:03:44.347
the gaps are what the
kit's tests observed.

62
00:03:44.347 --> 00:03:47.771
The compiled MOF records
the user name, host name,

63
00:03:47.771 --> 00:03:52.648
date and full script path, and that
MOF goes to a blob the machines read.

64
00:03:52.648 --> 00:03:57.330
Compiling writes each resource's
properties in a different order each run,

65
00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.747
so the hash changes with no real change.

66
00:03:59.747 --> 00:04:04.298
Packaging writes a different zip
each run, even from the same MOF.

67
00:04:04.298 --> 00:04:08.760
Nothing checks for secrets, although that
Learn guide says not to include them.

68
00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:13.005
The policy cmdlet takes whatever
policy I D and version it's given;

69
00:04:13.005 --> 00:04:17.958
Learn's page on creating policy definitions
says to reuse the I D for updates,

70
00:04:17.958 --> 00:04:20.710
and a new GUID creates
a second definition.

71
00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:25.085
And the generated definition leaves
Arc-enabled servers out by default.

72
00:04:25.085 --> 00:04:29.322
The build calls Learn's cmdlets in
Learn's order, and replaces none of them.

73
00:04:29.322 --> 00:04:31.750
What it adds sits between the steps.

74
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.922
The release registry is the kit's design:

75
00:04:37.922 --> 00:04:42.400
the reviewed record of what is in
production, one entry per configuration.

76
00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.675
The policy I D never changes.

77
00:04:44.675 --> 00:04:48.360
Learn's page on creating policy
definitions says to reuse it,

78
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.487
so updates replace the definition
instead of creating a second one.

79
00:04:52.487 --> 00:04:56.600
The mode is checked case-sensitively
against the three assignment types.

80
00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:01.278
The published version moves up in the
same pull request that publishes a

81
00:05:01.278 --> 00:05:05.800
new version, so a second build of one
point oh point oh fails MC-POL oh six.

82
00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:10.133
The identity is system, so each
machine reads the package with its own

83
00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:13.554
system-assigned identity,
which Learn's page on secure

84
00:05:13.554 --> 00:05:16.975
access to packages says works
for Arc-enabled servers.

85
00:05:16.975 --> 00:05:19.825
Including Arc machines is a stated choice.

86
00:05:19.825 --> 00:05:23.950
And the owner tag is the one this
workbook's Arc track guidance uses.

87
00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:28.384
An enforcing definition without
that filter fails MC-POL oh eight,

88
00:05:28.384 --> 00:05:33.970
because two tools on one setting flip it
back and forth, each reporting success.

89
00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:41.095
Three steps sit between Learn's,
and they're the kit's design.

90
00:05:41.095 --> 00:05:45.495
The cleaner rewrites the compiled
MOF into one canonical form.

91
00:05:45.495 --> 00:05:47.965
It drops the header comment, the author,

92
00:05:47.965 --> 00:05:50.820
the generation date and
the generation host.

93
00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:55.513
It keeps source info in the short
form Microsoft's own sample MOF uses,

94
00:05:55.513 --> 00:05:59.795
sorts each instance's properties,
and writes Unix line endings.

95
00:05:59.795 --> 00:06:02.994
Compiled three times,
the configuration gives

96
00:06:02.994 --> 00:06:05.782
three different MOFs, and one cleaned MOF.

97
00:06:05.782 --> 00:06:10.654
Then the package check opens the zip and
applies seven rules: the files at the root,

98
00:06:10.654 --> 00:06:12.253
the right type for the mode,

99
00:06:12.253 --> 00:06:17.124
the hundred-megabyte limit that Learn's
page on creating package artifacts sets,

100
00:06:17.124 --> 00:06:20.178
no build-host data, nothing
that looks like a secret,

101
00:06:20.178 --> 00:06:24.758
every module at the version it was compiled
against, and a version in the name.

102
00:06:24.758 --> 00:06:27.094
Then the definition check applies nine.

103
00:06:27.094 --> 00:06:30.467
The one that catches the
most is MC-POL oh two:

104
00:06:30.467 --> 00:06:35.230
every copy of the content hash
must be this package's hash.

105
00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.362
The build records what it
did in a provenance manifest:

106
00:06:42.362 --> 00:06:44.918
what was built, from
what, and what shipped.

107
00:06:44.918 --> 00:06:50.229
It holds the configuration, its version,
the fixed policy I D and the mode.

108
00:06:50.229 --> 00:06:55.305
It holds the MOF, the package and the
policy definition, each with its hash.

109
00:06:55.305 --> 00:06:58.510
It records the pinned module
versions the build used,

110
00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:02.080
and the counts of findings,
warnings and information items.

111
00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:03.967
There are two kinds of hash in it.

112
00:07:03.967 --> 00:07:06.332
The MOF hash is the reviewable one:

113
00:07:06.332 --> 00:07:09.793
it's the same for anyone
who builds the same commit.

114
00:07:09.793 --> 00:07:13.993
The package and definition hashes
identify the bytes that shipped.

115
00:07:13.993 --> 00:07:19.542
So an ISSO can download the published blob,
hash it, and match it against this file.

116
00:07:19.542 --> 00:07:25.410
Chapter 09, in Module 8, carries the
manifest into the evidence package.

117
00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:29.885
Try it.

118
00:07:29.885 --> 00:07:35.085
Lab 1 runs in the machine configuration
folder, in PowerShell 7 on Windows.

119
00:07:35.085 --> 00:07:38.635
Nothing signs in, uploads,
or installs the agent.

120
00:07:38.635 --> 00:07:42.002
Step one saves three pinned
modules from the PowerShell

121
00:07:42.002 --> 00:07:45.010
Gallery into a folder,
rather than installing them.

122
00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:50.342
Because of that download, the results on
these slides weren't re-run for this deck:

123
00:07:50.342 --> 00:07:53.250
they're the ones Chapter
07's Lab 1 text gives,

124
00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.835
as the path's verification log records.

125
00:07:55.835 --> 00:07:58.360
Step two follows Learn's steps by hand.

126
00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:02.485
Compile the configuration, and
package it twice from the same MOF.

127
00:08:02.485 --> 00:08:05.273
You get two packages with
two different hashes.

128
00:08:05.273 --> 00:08:09.781
Look inside the MOF, and your user name
and computer name are in the header,

129
00:08:09.781 --> 00:08:12.486
and your folder path is
in every source info.

130
00:08:12.486 --> 00:08:18.067
Step three runs the package check on
Learn's output: two MC-PKG oh four findings,

131
00:08:18.067 --> 00:08:20.946
for build-host data and an absolute path,

132
00:08:20.946 --> 00:08:24.610
and the oh seven warning,
no version in the name.

133
00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:32.222
Step four runs Microsoft's pinned
sample with the current module.

134
00:08:32.222 --> 00:08:36.776
Microsoft's sample, from the
Azure Policy samples repository,

135
00:08:36.776 --> 00:08:39.276
is pinned at an August 2021 commit,

136
00:08:39.276 --> 00:08:44.811
five years older than GuestConfiguration
4.12.0 on the PowerShell Gallery.

137
00:08:44.811 --> 00:08:48.571
Its MOF still packages, but
the cmdlet its script calls,

138
00:08:48.571 --> 00:08:53.335
test guest configuration package,
returns nothing: it's been renamed.

139
00:08:53.335 --> 00:08:57.423
Then check Microsoft's published
MOF with the kit's MOF check.

140
00:08:57.423 --> 00:09:00.933
It returns nothing, because
Microsoft's published MOF

141
00:09:00.933 --> 00:09:04.598
carries no build-host data,
the form the cleaner produces.

142
00:09:04.598 --> 00:09:07.227
That check needs none
of the gallery modules,

143
00:09:07.227 --> 00:09:09.998
and it printed nothing
again on November third.

144
00:09:09.998 --> 00:09:12.136
Step five runs the checked build.

145
00:09:12.136 --> 00:09:15.308
It exits zero, with one information row,

146
00:09:15.308 --> 00:09:19.672
MC-POL oh nine: include Arc
machines defaults to false.

147
00:09:19.672 --> 00:09:23.597
Build again into a second folder,
and compare the two manifests.

148
00:09:23.597 --> 00:09:28.216
The MOF hashes match, starting
e c f f d seven nine five,

149
00:09:28.216 --> 00:09:30.686
and the package hashes don't.

150
00:09:30.686 --> 00:09:33.890
So you publish the exact
bytes the build checked.

151
00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:41.227
Step six breaks the registry
on purpose, three ways.

152
00:09:41.227 --> 00:09:44.995
Each time, you write a copy of
the registry into the lab folder,

153
00:09:44.995 --> 00:09:46.728
and the build reads the copy.

154
00:09:46.728 --> 00:09:50.378
First, raise the published
version to one point oh point oh,

155
00:09:50.378 --> 00:09:52.127
the version you're building.

156
00:09:52.127 --> 00:09:57.377
The build exits two, MC-POL oh
six: the version didn't rise.

157
00:09:57.377 --> 00:10:02.328
Second, give the mode the wrong case,
apply and auto correct with a lower-case a.

158
00:10:02.328 --> 00:10:06.553
The build exits one, because the
assignment type is case sensitive.

159
00:10:06.553 --> 00:10:10.202
Third, fix the case, and
remove the owner tag.

160
00:10:10.202 --> 00:10:16.790
The build exits two, MC-POL oh eight: an
enforcing definition with no owner filter.

161
00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:20.815
Step seven runs the tests:
sixty-five pass with the modules,

162
00:10:20.815 --> 00:10:24.253
and forty-nine, with sixteen
skipped, without them.

163
00:10:24.253 --> 00:10:26.586
That second count needs no download,

164
00:10:26.586 --> 00:10:31.290
and on November third it gave
forty-nine passed and sixteen skipped.

165
00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:40.015
Lab 2 needs a test subscription and a
server you can rebuild, never production.

166
00:10:40.015 --> 00:10:44.736
Microsoft Learn's page, How to test
machine configuration package artifacts,

167
00:10:44.736 --> 00:10:48.351
says local testing installs
the agent and needs elevation,

168
00:10:48.351 --> 00:10:51.228
and remediation changes
the machine it runs on.

169
00:10:51.228 --> 00:10:53.878
Test the package on the disposable server.

170
00:10:53.878 --> 00:10:59.539
Publish the exact bytes Lab 1 built to a
storage account with anonymous access off.

171
00:10:59.539 --> 00:11:03.334
Grant the machines read access
with their own identity,

172
00:11:03.334 --> 00:11:05.890
so no SAS token goes in the definition.

173
00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:09.339
Publish the definition under
the registry's policy I D.

174
00:11:09.339 --> 00:11:12.328
Assign it with include
Arc machines set to true.

175
00:11:12.328 --> 00:11:17.002
And read each machine's compliance against
the hash in the provenance manifest.

176
00:11:17.002 --> 00:11:20.966
To back it out, step eight deletes
the assignment, the definition,

177
00:11:20.966 --> 00:11:25.303
the blob and the role assignment,
then rebuilds the disposable server.

178
00:11:25.303 --> 00:11:27.452
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

179
00:11:27.452 --> 00:11:30.350
Follow Chapter 07's Lab 2.

180
00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:35.487
The federal requirement.

181
00:11:35.487 --> 00:11:40.245
A package is the server baseline in
a file, so what was built, from what,

182
00:11:40.245 --> 00:11:45.681
and what shipped is the evidence for
NIST special publication eight hundred

183
00:11:45.681 --> 00:11:50.013
fifty-three controls such as S R
three, S A ten, S I seven and C M two.

184
00:11:50.013 --> 00:11:52.393
The pinned module versions support supply

185
00:11:52.393 --> 00:11:55.038
chain and developer
configuration management.

186
00:11:55.038 --> 00:11:59.938
The package check, refusing build-host
data, supports developer testing.

187
00:11:59.938 --> 00:12:02.940
The same commit giving
the same cleaned MOF hash,

188
00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:07.446
with the hashes identifying what
shipped, supports software integrity

189
00:12:07.446 --> 00:12:11.275
and the baseline, and the provenance
manifest is the evidence.

190
00:12:11.275 --> 00:12:14.447
The definition carries the
package's content hash,

191
00:12:14.447 --> 00:12:16.637
and Arc servers are a stated choice.

192
00:12:16.637 --> 00:12:20.798
A version that didn't rise, a mode
in the wrong case and an enforcing

193
00:12:20.798 --> 00:12:25.575
definition with no owner filter are
refused, in the pull request's check run.

194
00:12:25.575 --> 00:12:28.911
And Lab 2 adds the change
record for the published blob,

195
00:12:28.911 --> 00:12:30.838
and the role assignment record.

196
00:12:30.838 --> 00:12:35.771
The Key Security Indicators come from
FedRAMP's Consolidated Rules for 2026:

197
00:12:35.771 --> 00:12:39.009
they're FedRAMP 20x
indicators for providers,

198
00:12:39.009 --> 00:12:41.938
and the agency lines its
evidence up with them.

199
00:12:41.938 --> 00:12:45.870
Which output supports which
control is this workbook's reading.

200
00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:51.595
Check yourself with two questions.

201
00:12:51.595 --> 00:12:55.457
First: you run the package
cmdlet twice on the same MOF.

202
00:12:55.457 --> 00:12:56.383
What do you get?

203
00:12:56.383 --> 00:12:59.685
The answer is b: two packages
with different hashes,

204
00:12:59.685 --> 00:13:03.382
so a rebuilt package won't
match the reviewed definition.

205
00:13:03.382 --> 00:13:08.332
That's why the build packages once,
checks, and publishes those exact bytes.

206
00:13:08.332 --> 00:13:11.803
Second: you assign a definition
to an Arc-enabled server,

207
00:13:11.803 --> 00:13:14.670
and leave include Arc
machines at its default.

208
00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:15.682
What happens?

209
00:13:15.682 --> 00:13:19.495
The answer is b: nothing
happens, and nothing says so.

210
00:13:19.495 --> 00:13:24.174
The default is false, so every
Arc-enabled server is out of scope,

211
00:13:24.174 --> 00:13:27.683
and the build's MC-POL oh
nine row is the reminder.

212
00:13:27.683 --> 00:13:32.835
The module page has two more questions,
on MC-PKG oh four and on the mode's case,

213
00:13:32.835 --> 00:13:36.090
each linked to the
section that answers it.

214
00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:40.665
To sum up.

215
00:13:40.665 --> 00:13:44.482
You followed Learn's procedure,
saw where its outputs fall short,

216
00:13:44.482 --> 00:13:47.506
and met the build that makes
the same MOF every time,

217
00:13:47.506 --> 00:13:52.403
checks the package and the definition, and
refuses what the registry doesn't allow.

218
00:13:52.403 --> 00:13:54.163
The evidence an ISSO keeps:

219
00:13:54.163 --> 00:13:58.563
both provenance files and the
findings file in the evidence store,

220
00:13:58.563 --> 00:14:03.283
the registry and its pull requests, and
in Lab 2, the published blob's hash,

221
00:14:03.283 --> 00:14:07.043
the role assignment, the
definition's activity log entry,

222
00:14:07.043 --> 00:14:10.803
and each machine's compliance
against the recorded hash.

223
00:14:10.803 --> 00:14:14.552
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 07 checklist.

224
00:14:14.552 --> 00:14:19.066
Next is Module 6: onboard
servers with Ansible beside Arc.

225
00:14:19.066 --> 00:14:22.799
You'll read a CMDB export into
groups and tags, plan a wave,

226
00:14:22.799 --> 00:14:27.170
and see what each server would
get before anything is contacted.
